WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.185
Choose a position that can support both steadiness and ease.

2
00:00:06.885 --> 00:00:11.715
Let the eyes rest open or closed.

3
00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:18.420
Feel one place where the body meets a surface.

4
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:31.195
There is no need to clear the mind before beginning; whatever is already present can be the starting point.

5
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.400
Notice one breath.

6
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.575
Feel it at the nose, chest, belly, or wherever it is most available.

7
00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:55.165
Let this simple sensation be home base for now.

8
00:01:04.972 --> 00:01:09.272
Sooner or later, attention will move.

9
00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:17.657
It may follow a thought, a sound, a feeling, or nothing you can name.

10
00:01:19.357 --> 00:01:24.062
This movement is not an interruption of the practice.

11
00:01:25.762 --> 00:01:30.862
The instant you recognize it, awareness has already returned.

12
00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:51.396
Try the return in three small steps.

13
00:01:53.096 --> 00:02:03.001
First, acknowledge what was happening: thinking, hearing, remembering, planning.

14
00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:09.266
Second, feel the body supported.

15
00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:14.886
Third, meet the next breath.

16
00:02:16.586 --> 00:02:21.126
There is no need to push the previous experience away.

17
00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:44.574
Stay with breathing until attention moves again.

18
00:02:46.274 --> 00:02:50.114
Do not wait tensely for wandering.

19
00:02:51.814 --> 00:02:56.934
Let the breath be ordinary and let the mind behave like a mind.

20
00:03:23.085 --> 00:03:32.400
When you notice you have left, listen for the tone you use with yourself.

21
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:48.015
If it sounds sharp or disappointed, see whether a more factual phrase is possible: attention moved, and now it is here.

22
00:03:49.715 --> 00:03:51.915
Return through the body and breath.

23
00:04:11.528 --> 00:04:13.648
You may notice repeated thoughts.

24
00:04:15.348 --> 00:04:20.333
A thought can return many times without becoming a command.

25
00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:27.023
Each time, recognize it briefly and choose the anchor again.

26
00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:33.588
The number of returns is not evidence of failure.

27
00:04:35.288 --> 00:04:40.303
It is the number of moments in which the practice became visible.

28
00:05:09.722 --> 00:05:17.667
Now choose a different anchor: perhaps the hands, the feet, or the sounds in the room.

29
00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:25.127
Notice that returning does not depend on one special sensation.

30
00:05:26.827 --> 00:05:32.092
Any clear, present experience can offer a way back.

31
00:05:54.974 --> 00:05:59.919
If attention feels restless, make the next interval shorter.

32
00:06:01.619 --> 00:06:06.754
Stay for one breath, then deliberately listen.

33
00:06:08.454 --> 00:06:14.009
Feel one contact point, then deliberately look.

34
00:06:15.709 --> 00:06:22.239
Moving attention with intention can restore steadiness without forcing stillness.

35
00:06:45.121 --> 00:06:47.041
Come back to the original anchor.

36
00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:54.551
Notice whether it is exactly as you remember it.

37
00:06:56.251 --> 00:07:00.081
The breath may be lighter or stronger.

38
00:07:01.781 --> 00:07:03.381
The contact may have shifted.

39
00:07:05.081 --> 00:07:14.531
Returning means meeting what is here now, not recreating what was here before.

40
00:07:40.682 --> 00:07:45.387
For the next minute, let the practice be mostly quiet.

41
00:07:47.087 --> 00:07:58.677
Each time you become aware of wandering, use the three steps: acknowledge, feel support, meet one breath.

42
00:08:37.903 --> 00:08:39.703
Release the deliberate anchor.

43
00:08:41.403 --> 00:08:45.563
Let awareness widen to the full body and room.

44
00:08:47.263 --> 00:08:48.743
Thoughts can continue.

45
00:08:50.443 --> 00:08:52.043
Sounds can continue.

46
00:08:53.743 --> 00:08:58.393
The body can keep breathing without your management.

47
00:09:11.468 --> 00:09:13.708
Consider what made return possible.

48
00:09:15.408 --> 00:09:17.808
It was not perfect focus.

49
00:09:19.508 --> 00:09:26.218
It was the ability to notice, to orient, and to begin again without punishment.

50
00:09:36.025 --> 00:09:41.075
Let the eyes focus and make the next movement slowly.

51
00:09:42.775 --> 00:09:52.535
When attention leaves during the rest of the day, you do not have to start the whole day over.

52
00:09:54.235 --> 00:10:00.000
One clear contact and one ordinary breath can be enough for a gentle return.
